le. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{5}{9},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/488ff32187c9a9a335e3782fb717242d899f622c43010662e28edcabe0e1bde3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{5}{9}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{9}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{5}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{25}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{56}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-65059/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-65059"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124625/selection/44faf3f4981ab5bbc45f9e62d9e86b239a431bda0e13f141810fea4388d975af" :answer/id #uuid "4ec8601e-121f-5a16-a1ac-a3a519551281" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124625/selection/a6158f066e665d704064059fe28a40ebed39b538a6b11bbee085c6998bb52f58" :answer/id #uuid "b2a8f537-0410-55c3-b01d-3ec8a378925c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124625/selection/9e0a05d08304fe0895a7fa5a7f5676749a02d223aa2e992f27c0067a04861357" :answer/id #uuid "5d3498e8-5a0b-5310-9e17-0b5cd1b84d3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124625/selection/d3a7133ef1a1f6526aaec763d522f5ae075d8386a4606de90dcb93094f1d21d6" :answer/id #uuid "03938b3a-cc46-5bd0-b4fc-3e78c371893b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124625/selection/7b90ad80455272770675a6cc5d2461eafee4c1e9e9d4f5e8dcf9b8b7434b1987" :answer/id #uuid "a5c23f4c-182f-5bad-95e7-a406b53f20ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124625/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "559a3139-758d-5080-9b05-e954fd554690" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124625/selection/9e0a05d08304fe0895a7fa5a7f5676749a02d223aa2e992f27c0067a04861357" :answer-field/choices ["q-124625/selection/44faf3f4981ab5bbc45f9e62d9e86b239a431bda0e13f141810fea4388d975af" "q-124625/selection/a6158f066e665d704064059fe28a40ebed39b538a6b11bbee085c6998bb52f58" "q-124625/selection/9e0a05d08304fe0895a7fa5a7f5676749a02d223aa2e992f27c0067a04861357" "q-124625/selection/d3a7133ef1a1f6526aaec763d522f5ae075d8386a4606de90dcb93094f1d21d6" "q-124625/selection/7b90ad80455272770675a6cc5d2461eafee4c1e9e9d4f5e8dcf9b8b7434b1987"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124625" :question/id #uuid "ab93d133-79c0-514a-b98d-36a4fd849be1" :question/math-academy-id "q-124625" :question/is-example false :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/e50a755a20330d7f0eca8d02c8ddab33f392d5b70f7ca35b4dc514e22e61141c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{6}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/fb0da765a857607ce0c8f13690efaa2f0d504ccaa51899a23044ee4ed17f0aa5.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{6}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{6}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{6}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{6}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{36}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-124625/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124625"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-65064/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "13bc81d1-21a6-5e6b-8669-18095376079a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65064/selection/5fd45cec95fc45f99550587cbe4d80dda358c751536fdf37920e2dff6de9568a" :answer/id #uuid "071315d9-a698-5cdb-9149-7474f93cc974" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65064/selection/219347bb4a6f51134d7f5aff5da33544f44afee934064621ebff9cf9e335ee59" :answer/id #uuid "f5888265-a507-5860-9981-8afecb17c71e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65064/selection/91dbd277ab4fea5d11caa2c19f3dcef0075171f14883f8fa2a8294d63129a69a" :answer/id #uuid "9ec19daf-d813-5220-8c1e-f9cca3e1f725" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65064/selection/792e4603c3cdfaae400be056328702ef30f8064851fe81d040710db9424c8ade" :answer/id #uuid "5dbfbd72-cb35-5d5b-bb60-1742fdfed611" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65064/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "416ca512-9b3e-51ce-9dd8-4cc34baee168" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-65064/selection/792e4603c3cdfaae400be056328702ef30f8064851fe81d040710db9424c8ade" :answer-field/choices ["q-65064/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-65064/selection/5fd45cec95fc45f99550587cbe4d80dda358c751536fdf37920e2dff6de9568a" "q-65064/selection/219347bb4a6f51134d7f5aff5da33544f44afee934064621ebff9cf9e335ee59" "q-65064/selection/91dbd277ab4fea5d11caa2c19f3dcef0075171f14883f8fa2a8294d63129a69a" "q-65064/selection/792e4603c3cdfaae400be056328702ef30f8064851fe81d040710db9424c8ade"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-65064" :question/id #uuid "a9fcc86d-d9ab-5e33-bf4c-8062ae1074d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-65064" :question/is-example false :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/455df038e550d78fc29463c1a2e22dd44838d24af7428a824f9046fecc1ac1db.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $csc⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/44a555d222e3f6ff85208e8722a728ad240c558de7982d2a480bf9ddfb23ac6f.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{15}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5})}=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}csc⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-65064/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "435bc6f9-651f-511c-a972-9afe90baff32"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-65064"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-65062/selection/7028c4ccf6c825a35feaf390bb3300b9a488d03bb5142dfd06e85577fc8094d8" :answer/id #uuid "ec2cb4be-53e8-5794-be39-15402508ff13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65062/selection/781fe82debeff6f6822e95b35a59892765a5535ea2b78e7cf40af6b177cb8cef" :answer/id #uuid "cf1e54a3-2591-57be-a6be-9e6929fdb0c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65062/selection/2be68e7683286e4f73bc0003ba1da9f176e9e2abe59822b35983d4cf8e1449e2" :answer/id #uuid "27f109b8-195d-57da-a72e-11244a6c22c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65062/selection/2e322c183dbc7d1ea89480e5179cfacfe0785b47fba2faa443ef41c049164964" :answer/id #uuid "10b0291a-472e-5a71-bf30-084931436f2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65062/selection/de67724e28e3ffc563c9d92aefa0f82f887fc73341d69574b61f499bf9cceb87" :answer/id #uuid "028f5505-8e3d-5295-bdbd-78e879ac34bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65062/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "804831b1-c89f-52c2-9a7b-85aaaabda928" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-65062/selection/2e322c183dbc7d1ea89480e5179cfacfe0785b47fba2faa443ef41c049164964" :answer-field/choices ["q-65062/selection/7028c4ccf6c825a35feaf390bb3300b9a488d03bb5142dfd06e85577fc8094d8" "q-65062/selection/781fe82debeff6f6822e95b35a59892765a5535ea2b78e7cf40af6b177cb8cef" "q-65062/selection/2be68e7683286e4f73bc0003ba1da9f176e9e2abe59822b35983d4cf8e1449e2" "q-65062/selection/2e322c183dbc7d1ea89480e5179cfacfe0785b47fba2faa443ef41c049164964" "q-65062/selection/de67724e28e3ffc563c9d92aefa0f82f887fc73341d69574b61f499bf9cceb87"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-65062" :question/id #uuid "8e36e5e3-f31b-56d5-ac07-708bf215be4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-65062" :question/is-example false :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/43629ebfaa2f9811135ffb15f5e5a59b796b83e3a525b4ab480efb9840d40879.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sec⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/f5219a363cdf18cfb628148ca7b9bbe6015b521799ed4487fa9b56843eaa3043.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{3}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{6}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}.$\n\nKnowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3})}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-65062/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "435bc6f9-651f-511c-a972-9afe90baff32"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-65062"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q