swer-fields ["q-85234/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "435bc6f9-651f-511c-a972-9afe90baff32"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-85234"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124743/selection/16aebcc0f6d564cc83b305837e1b46e82b83ddec7178ee48b4217b6c6fdd025a" :answer/id #uuid "6c0e3508-e882-50e4-bc97-242e3a47e51b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124743/selection/8af3f47da5b1fad81039a366cce980fe9ac2dfd372cf746c5bf32d603cfe8469" :answer/id #uuid "008e69dd-b5c9-5869-a946-4d21571bbf31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124743/selection/5fb6be176de14df07c89e2bd4e2ececf4dc87d2f6d903730bb91fd1a73dfeb8a" :answer/id #uuid "173a3a92-a23d-55ac-ae5e-2ea5f6d52636" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124743/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "f2e511f7-c7c4-5b50-8c8c-557644bf0e41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124743/selection/f52409178afa6605d44eeec7644d28d3cde5bf5b850da6e6e545f0ac20ada3d9" :answer/id #uuid "833f6792-a8a1-51cd-9cc9-b82ee54e2be3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124743/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2b6f2673-fb9a-57f1-993c-5c1d4a842e4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124743/selection/8af3f47da5b1fad81039a366cce980fe9ac2dfd372cf746c5bf32d603cfe8469" :answer-field/choices ["q-124743/selection/16aebcc0f6d564cc83b305837e1b46e82b83ddec7178ee48b4217b6c6fdd025a" "q-124743/selection/8af3f47da5b1fad81039a366cce980fe9ac2dfd372cf746c5bf32d603cfe8469" "q-124743/selection/5fb6be176de14df07c89e2bd4e2ececf4dc87d2f6d903730bb91fd1a73dfeb8a" "q-124743/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" "q-124743/selection/f52409178afa6605d44eeec7644d28d3cde5bf5b850da6e6e545f0ac20ada3d9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124743" :question/id #uuid "2ae77c0b-34b8-5a79-a5be-337b58c15915" :question/math-academy-id "q-124743" :question/is-example false :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/02cb693b74f0f315389f87ceed1674da11f661b3363e4df0890bdf82d61cc60c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $csc⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/a479e8e7906a3a0dc43e925c5bccd6534b4ab2e02a289403b2d0c92d0b0eb1d9.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$\n\nKnowing that $csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ncsc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})}=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}csc⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-124743/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "435bc6f9-651f-511c-a972-9afe90baff32"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124743"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-124750"] :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/734eef45985ddfb6450666e1dd32c6d3beab9ca3aef66c87c6ba393d6960b08b.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{1}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{24}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5})}=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{2\\cdot 6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-65073"] :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/21170c11aa5ff0ab39b86f94588e5e8a0fa178d0c34b9aa1774ee9f07a4d0812.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{45}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})}{(\\frac{2}{7})}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-65072/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" :answer/id #uuid "64a8d36d-4f29-54df-ad2e-2faf91badf75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65072/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer/id #uuid "4cd2c4fb-a25a-5040-aa50-9f7a883f5ac8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65072/