eference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/5284ae5a30a5d370d52c887cc6222ee4991a98d087dc751cbcfe5d7307ca1d47.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{17}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{64}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{8}{9}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $cot⁡\\theta =\\frac{cos⁡\\theta }{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9})}{(\\frac{8}{9})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{8}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-65075/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74d05ad6-7e4a-5c50-92d1-ca4b2f00cbb1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-65075"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124791/selection/aad510c3a39c3e9d7adec3d96bd7567a16544a9f4be8deb2f5e95d47b24522bd" :answer/id #uuid "10e2511c-8111-59c0-bf2d-bb44bd2b3b33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124791/selection/bf569fde3bf7671d9b5605e4b0b0b0a558f1fc462a30f36c882701dc075efc09" :answer/id #uuid "72587a39-f71b-5281-bf1b-c1e7029df0ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124791/selection/5ca6df5a251f695d50a389dd4918248ad78ac0a41de4d04c01451e32e01aad28" :answer/id #uuid "c32b75c8-8b4d-59cc-8c26-962e22445c27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124791/selection/ec1a315a9d116f286c99fc68c51aad9fd7a4fb8bdb4ca94b96fcecc775f1daa9" :answer/id #uuid "727628e1-2b2c-52db-89eb-d88d368bdee6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124791/selection/53be14eed81838a33f0ffce24087c37fa89d53f16b6af253d4532b085d8c80bf" :answer/id #uuid "b12489f9-47f5-5b99-a3f9-827300c18a51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124791/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "35b84333-5685-56f4-8cb6-f32ebe08d3d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124791/selection/bf569fde3bf7671d9b5605e4b0b0b0a558f1fc462a30f36c882701dc075efc09" :answer-field/choices ["q-124791/selection/aad510c3a39c3e9d7adec3d96bd7567a16544a9f4be8deb2f5e95d47b24522bd" "q-124791/selection/bf569fde3bf7671d9b5605e4b0b0b0a558f1fc462a30f36c882701dc075efc09" "q-124791/selection/5ca6df5a251f695d50a389dd4918248ad78ac0a41de4d04c01451e32e01aad28" "q-124791/selection/ec1a315a9d116f286c99fc68c51aad9fd7a4fb8bdb4ca94b96fcecc775f1daa9" "q-124791/selection/53be14eed81838a33f0ffce24087c37fa89d53f16b6af253d4532b085d8c80bf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124791" :question/id #uuid "31a24d95-250d-52f5-aac3-20c4a9ac5e80" :question/math-academy-id "q-124791" :question/is-example false :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/c2938adc4474cec0da06b5cee00b60110b7e7be86d2b0cec3f1cff99653b8f76.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5},$ what is $tan⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/af76b605cad900f45bc9def13e9ec2ffb12cb253ff0bdd4c07e764eac2a30937.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{12}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5})}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13\\cdot 3}}{2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-124791/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74d05ad6-7e4a-5c50-92d1-ca4b2f00cbb1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124791"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124789/selection/b9eb51e441ee30195dc83b48f69c51745ebf5bc4c29ef9ea23d90fe820a52304" :answer/id #uuid "44d5d213-4d0a-52ee-bc34-4cc787301